# omarchy-moat: post-install guidance only.
#
# This file never enables, starts or restarts a unit. Arch's own alpm hooks
# already handle the parts that can be automated:
#   /usr/share/libalpm/hooks/20-systemd-sysusers.hook -> systemd-sysusers
#   /usr/share/libalpm/hooks/21-systemd-tmpfiles.hook -> systemd-tmpfiles --create
#   /usr/share/libalpm/hooks/30-systemd-daemon-reload-system.hook
# so the `moat` group, the `moat-ship` user, /var/lib/moat, /var/lib/moat/ship,
# /var/log/moat and /run/moat exist by the time pacman returns. Running them here would only duplicate work.

post_install() {
	cat <<'EOF'

  Moat is installed but not running. Next steps:

    1. sudo systemctl enable --now tetragon moatd moat-feeds.timer
    2. sudo usermod -aG moat $USER
    3. Log out and back in (the new group only applies to a fresh session).
    4. omarchy plugin add https://github.com/the2dl/omarchy-moat --enable
    5. systemctl --user enable --now moat-digest.timer moat-triage.timer

  Step 5 enables two *user* timers -- run them as yourself, without sudo.

  moat-digest.timer is the weekly digest: one normal-urgency notification a week
  ("moat: 0 incidents, 18 installs watched, 3 baseline proposals to review")
  and the only scheduled notification Moat ever sends. It is a user timer
  because desktop notifications belong to your session. Turn the content off
  with `moatctl set digest off` (the timer then delivers nothing), or drop the
  timer entirely with `systemctl --user disable --now moat-digest.timer`.

  moat-triage.timer hands each new alert to your default coding agent for a
  plain-language verdict, every ten minutes. It sends no notifications; it
  annotates alerts in the panel. The agent can explain an alert, propose an
  allowlist block for you to accept, and -- on a confident "benign" -- move an
  alert from the badge to the timeline. It can never hide, acknowledge, delete
  or re-severity one, and `moatctl triage --undo <id>` reverses a demotion.
  Set `[analysis] auto_triage = "annotate"` in /etc/moat/moat.toml to keep the
  verdicts but stop the demotions, or `"off"` to stop it entirely; without a
  default agent (`omarchy default agent claude`) it does nothing.

  OPTIONAL: tune moat by talking to your coding agent.

    The package installs an agent skill at
    /usr/share/omarchy/default/agents/skills/moat-tuning. Omarchy links the
    skills in that directory into each agent's skill directory, but it does
    so from a one-shot migration -- so on a machine that is already set up,
    link it yourself, as you, once:

      ln -sfn /usr/share/omarchy/default/agents/skills/moat-tuning \
              ~/.claude/skills/moat-tuning

    (or ~/.gemini/config/skills/ for Gemini). The skill teaches an agent
    which tuning layer a change belongs in -- kernel policy, kernel
    exclusion, userspace allowlist, threshold, or enforcement -- and why
    naming a shell script in a kernel policy is recorded, reloaded, verified
    and completely inert. It reads; it proposes; it does not act.

  Then check it:

    moatctl status

  Moat starts in monitor mode: it alerts, it never kills. Switch with
  `moatctl set mode enforce`. The bubblewrap shims are off until
  `moatctl set sandbox on`, which needs a new login shell to take effect.

  Config lives in /etc/moat; docs in /usr/share/doc/omarchy-moat.

  OPTIONAL, and off until you say otherwise:

    Telemetry classes. Moat records `alerts` and nothing else by default. It
    can also record `process` (exec/exit), `network` (outbound connects) and
    `file` (executable-shaped writes and chmod +x) into
    /var/lib/moat/telemetry.jsonl. `moatd telemetry` prints what is on and
    what each class costs; the measured numbers for this shape of machine are
    in SHIPPING.md section 4. Turning `network` or `file` on changes what the
    KERNEL is asked to do, so it needs new policies and a sensor restart:

      sudoedit /etc/moat/moat.toml      # the [telemetry] block
      sudo moatd telemetry --apply

    That command validates, renders, re-runs the policy checker, restarts
    tetragon then moatd in that order, and then VERIFIES the sensor came back
    by counting what the kernel actually pinned. Do not restart by hand: a bad
    policy load is a silent outage, and on 2026-09-03 one cost 25 minutes of
    blindness while every surface still read "running".

    Shipping. moat-ship sends alerts and telemetry to an HTTPS collector
    (Splunk HEC, Elastic, Loki, Datadog) or to syslog. It runs as its own
    unprivileged user, reads only the two 0640 root:moat log files, redacts
    $HOME and credential filenames by default, and will not ship staged
    evidence at all.

      sudoedit /etc/moat/ship.toml
      moat-ship check                 # validate; sends nothing
      moat-ship once --dry-run        # print exactly what a collector gets
      sudo systemctl enable --now moat-ship

    See SHIPPING.md. Note the heartbeat: alert on its ABSENCE at your
    collector, or a shipper that has died looks exactly like a quiet machine.

EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
	cat <<'EOF'

  Moat was upgraded. Nothing was restarted for you.

  Tetragon reads its tracing policies once, at startup, and has no hot-reload,
  so new or changed policies are not live until you restart it. moatd
  likewise loads its config and policy annotations at start:

    sudo systemctl restart tetragon moatd

  In that order, and as one command: moatd.service is Requires=+After=
  tetragon.service, so restarting moatd first would leave it reading a log
  that the old Tetragon is still writing with the old policy set, and
  restarting tetragon alone leaves moatd holding the previous policies'
  annotations -- alerts would carry the old titles, severities and `why`
  text for policies that no longer exist. Restarting tetragon re-runs
  `moatd render-policies`, which rewrites /run/moat/policies and
  /etc/tetragon/tetragon.conf.d/export-allowlist; moatd picks the rendered
  set up on its own start (or on `systemctl reload moatd`, which is SIGHUP).

  If you enabled the weekly digest, the user timer needs nothing: it only
  runs `moatctl digest --notify`, which talks to whatever moatd is running.

  If you have telemetry classes on, use `sudo moatd telemetry --apply` instead
  of the restart above: it re-runs the policy checker over the newly rendered
  policies and verifies afterwards that the sensor came back loaded.

  If moat-ship is enabled, restart it too so it picks up the new binary:

    sudo systemctl restart moat-ship

  Its cursor is durable, so nothing is re-sent and nothing is skipped.

  Check for .pacnew files under /etc/moat and /etc/tetragon if you have
  edited the shipped configuration.

EOF
}

pre_remove() {
	cat <<'EOF'

  Disable the services before removing the package, or systemd will keep
  failed units around:

    sudo systemctl disable --now tetragon moatd moat-feeds.timer moat-ship
    systemctl --user disable --now moat-digest.timer moat-triage.timer

  /var/lib/moat (alerts, quarantine, feeds, incidents) and /var/log/moat
  are not removed by pacman; delete them by hand if you want them gone.

EOF
}
